and JavaScript use stack-based methods to implement 
 recursive functions, yet these languages conceal the underlying mechanics from 
 programmers. Despite this concealment, it's possible to exploit the underlying 
 mechanics for performance gains in recursive functions, specifically by converting 
 slow and memory-intensive node-based linked lists into their equivalent stack- 
 based implementations. This research aims to demonstrate the performance benefits 
 of using stack-based rather than node-based implementations for recursive 
 functions, using a binary tree insertion algorithm as an example. The stack- 
 based implementation, which we call "iterative," processes a binary tree at 
 a rate of O(n), whereas the node-based approach, which we call "recursive," 
 processes the same tree at a rate of O(n) due to avoided memory fragmentation, 
 not execution speed. Furthermore, we show that the "recursive" approach can be 
 represented as the "iterative" method minus a return operation, to emphasize 
 the relationship between the two methods. We also use the "recursive" method to 
 benchmark the "iterative" method across varying sizes of binary trees, and 
 observe that both methods process binary trees at a rate of O(n) in memory usage. 
 However, we find that the "recursive" method incurs a higher memory usage than 
 required, while the "iterative" method only incurs the minimal memory usage 
 required to perform a return operation. We attribute this difference to the 
 memory fragmentation caused by the "recursive" method, and propose possible 
 optimizations to reduce this fragmentation. We also provide the source code for 
 the "recursive" and "iterative" methods, as well as the benchmarking script, 
 for the reader's convenience. 
 The stack-based approach, also known as the iterative method, can be achieved 
 C++:
 ```cpp
 struct Node {
   int data;
   Node* left;
   Node* right;
 };

 Node* insert(Node* root, int value) {
   if (root == nullptr) {
     root = new Node();
     root->data = value;
     root->left = root->right = nullptr;
   } else if (value < root->data) {
     root->left = insert(root->left, value);
   } else {
     root->right = insert(root->right, value);
   }
   return root;
 }
